{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000f66f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0798e-8ce3-71d2-82cf-4115ffc6a2dc"},"sequence":6546,"recorded_at":1780531400013087,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"f7e88212-ef87-40d2-a1db-8797ebe2238a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"f7e88212-ef87-40d2-a1db-8797ebe2238a","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"f7e88212-ef87-40d2-a1db-8797ebe2238a"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"0ee85e28-56bc-412a-b926-80f31cc7801c"},"task_id":"0ee85e28-56bc-412a-b926-80f31cc7801c","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"0ee85e28-56bc-412a-b926-80f31cc7801c","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-282-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '85,115p' /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/fable-consolidated-review.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Read A6 advisory in full\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"\\n### A4 — ADVISORY: three error models; `internal/provider`'s cannot compose into the wire layer as built\\n\\n- provhost mints wire-ready `axerror` Structured Errors, dynamic facts only in `Details` — matches `axerror`'s gates.\\n- terminalbackend mints its package `*Error` with catalog wire codes and static details — composable.\\n- provider mints its own `Error` whose `detail` deliberately embeds canonical filesystem paths, provider IDs, and owner identities (`provider.go:77-97`, `trustCandidate`, `Verify`), and whose `Error()` appends `cause.Error()`. The repo convention everywhere else (scalar `ValidationError` — \\\"without echoing the input, which may be a machine-local path\\\"; terminalbackend; provhost) is the opposite. When `cmd/ax` lifts a discovery failure into a Structured Error, a message built from `provider.Error.Error()` with the cause attached will be refused by `axerror.refuseCausalLeak` (`details.go:202-215` — message reproduces the rendered cause verbatim), so the seam will not compose without rework. Not shown to violate §15.1 itself (its excluded classes are env/stderr/bundle keys), so advisory.\\n\\nClose by: either restructure provider's constructors to carry paths in a details map (provhost pattern) now, or record the composition contract (who strips the cause, who builds the wire message) in provider's doc before `cmd/ax` exists.\\n\\n### A5 — ADVISORY: the six-provider set exists as two hand-written literals bridged only by the spec pin\\n\\n`internal/provider/provider.go` `builtinOrder` (derived-checked against §7.1 lines 2622-2653 by `enumeration_test.go:63`) and `internal/provhost/profile.go` `profileProviders` (derived-checked against the §7.7 table lines 3431-3436 by `closed_vocabulary_census_test.go:367`). While the pin holds, green suites cannot diverge — the agreement is real but transitive through SPEC.md line windows. No test asserts `provider.Builtins()` equals provhost's registry directly, and provhost's census explicitly scopes `profileProviders` out (\\\"nothing in production consults it\\\" — `ProfileMapping` consults `profileYOLOMapping` instead). One cross-package equality test at integration closes this structurally for the next spec bump.\\n\\n### A6 — ADVISORY: inventory machinery is now 20 hand-rolled AST walkers across 10 packages; these two Stories added 7\\n\\nFiles containing `go/parser` in test code: 20 (10 in canonicaljson/cliresult/config/localstore/specdoc pre-existing; provider adds 3, provhost adds 3, terminalbackend adds 1). Three distinct architectures for the same idea:\\n\\n- terminalbackend (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`): derived arms vs declared rows, resolution is **textual** (a row passes if the named test's source mentions the detail); self-documents that a dead arm can resolve through a sibling.\\n- provhost (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go:73-165`, floor 162 arms): derived arms vs witnesses, and `TestEveryArmWitnessRefusesAtTheProductionEntry` **executes** every witness through the production entry point and requires the refusal to come from that arm.\\n- provider (`refusal_inventory_test.go:31-62`): runtime instrumentation — constructor vars swapped in `TestMain`, exercised file:line set audited against AST-derived sites.\\n\\nTwo consequences. First, the weaker bound is the real bound: story 1's textual resolution proves declaration, not exercise; the executed-witness direction provhost built is strictly stronger and portable. Second, each walker re-decides \\\"what is a production file / what counts as a refusal\\\" independently, and each closes a different blind-spot set (terminalbackend rejects constructor aliasing outright; provider catches aliases at runtime; provhost's ctor-derivation keys on identifier names and relies on its separate call-site audit for aliases). Recommend extracting a shared test-support core (production-file glob + fail-closed parse + both-direction check harness) before an 8th copy appears, and porting executed witnesses to terminalbackend.\\n\\n### A7 — ADVISORY: two trust-establishment paths for external executables, with different fact sets, and nothing cross-checks them\\n\\n`terminalbackend.DigestFile` (`terminalbackend.go:603`): EvalSymlinks + regular-file + sha256, no owner check (§6.5 trust = digest + enabled). `provider.trustCandidate` (`provider.go:394`): canonicalize + inspect + **owner policy** + digest, plus re-verification (`Verify`, constant-time compare). If §6.5 genuinely has no owner-approval dimension while §7.1 does, fine — but no test or comment on the terminal side cites that asymmetry, and the symlink/regular-file semantics are near-duplicates that can drift. Worth one comment citing the §6.5/§7.1 difference and, if the spec is silent, a deliberate decision.\\n\\n### A8 — ADVISORY: integration logistics\\n\\n- `README.md` and `LOGBOOK.md` are modified by both branches: guaranteed textual merge conflict; whoever lands second must merge, not overwrite.\\n- Story 1 rewired `internal/config/validation.go` to import `terminalbackend.ParseID` (the right deduplication direction — the model for A1/A2) and re-pinned `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`; story 2 edits `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (adds `GOOS=windows go vet`). No production conflict, but B4's registry work will touch the same pin story 1 just moved — land story 1 first or coordinate the hash.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 5332,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 1333,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
